OR"] " / 13 February 2010 / " [:a {:href "/records/event_name/545"} "Details"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "It was difficult to single out a favorite for this event. Four different racers had won the World Cups earlier in the season: No Jin-Kyu, " [:a {:href "/athletes/109918"} "Charles Hamelin"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/128113"} "Lee Han-Bin"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/119765"} "J. R. Celski"] ". No, also World Champion in 2011 and 2012, was missing in Sochi due to a broken elbow, but the other three all were there. The 2006 Olympic Champion, An Hyeon-Su, was also considered for a medal, although the former Korean now skated for Russia as " [:a {:href "/athletes/101557"} "Viktor An"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "All the favored athletes reached the final, although in Lee’s case it was as a result of being impeded in the semi-final. There was drama in the final when Briton " [:a {:href "/athletes/119766"} "Jack Whelbourne"] ", a surprise finalist, hit one of the rubber blocks and twisted his ankle. As the race progressed Hamelin always stayed near the front and came through to take the gold medal, with " [:a {:href "/athletes/127578"} "Han Tianyu"] " just edging out An for the silver medal. Lee was Korea’s best performer, by their standards a disappointing result in finishing sixth. An won Russia their first ever short-track medal"] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Helmet Nr"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Position Last Race"] [:th {} "Positions"] [:th {} "Best Time"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "205"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/109918"} "Charles Hamelin"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/CAN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/CAN.png"}] "CAN"]] [:td {} "AF-1"] [:td {} "1-1"] [:td {} "2:14.480"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "G